Seems that the current endgame will be impossible for many classes.
These limitations don't seem natural to me.  Wizards don't naturally have much
armor or weapons, so they don't start the game with such things.  But
a wizard would of course keep a good sword or mail if he found one!
Same goes for other classes.  A barbarian would certainly appreciate the 
instant kill effect of a wand of death...

If more differences between the classes is desired, make the characters
having problems with using items that they aren't supposed to use.
Spellbooks already work this way, as they depend on intelligence and
magical power.  Other items could be made more class-dependent as well.
Examples:

Barbarians and others unfamiliar with magic:  
These types sometimes spill a potion when trying to quaff or #dip.  Throwing
would still work, as that is weapon use.  Wand-zapping would go
in the wrong direction now and then, or just not work.  Identifying
new wands/scrolls/spellbooks by using them (rather than using identify)
should fail often, and perhaps confuse the player.
These things should of course change to the better if the player manages to
raise his intelligence & wisdom.

Wizards and others unfamiliar with normal fighting:
Sometimes armor don't fit well.  "These boots are too small, etc." The armor
will either be impossible to wear or give less protection than usual, or
in rare cases cause fumbling.  This should be decided for each piece of armor,
not for armor types.  It should be possible to get real good armor, but it
would be more work.  (Try on average 5 uncursed orcish helms before finding
one that works...)
These types aren't trained in weapon use, so each time they change to a new
weapon type, it will take some "training time" before the weapon performs
normally.  The training period for a weapon class would be over after using
the weapon type x times.  In the training period, the player sometimes fumble,
miss more than usual, and cause less damage. 

Some items are class specific, and other classes would have problems using
them, (but they might learn...)  Examples:
camera: tourists, stetoscope: healers, lock picking: rogues, 
pick-axe: archeologists, magic lamps: wizards

priests should be better at converting altars, they are used to religious things.

